  "updated": "2026-11-26T09:33:48.879513+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong low pressure system over the region will\ncontinue to bring wind and rain to the area today. \n\nA strengthening low pressure system will move up the east coast\ntoday, headed for the Canadian Maritime this weekend. As the\nstorm gains latitude it will deepen to near 996 MB as it tracks\nits way up the I-95 corridor through eastern VA today. The \nstrong pressure gradient associated with this feature will \nproduce some stealthy winds in addition to a large footprint of \nsoaking rainfall. \n\nRain has been ongoing across our forecast area since late\nWednesday with the heaviest amounts observed across the\npiedmont. Additional rain today will be focused over the\npiedmont and into the Virginia Blue Ridge from Floyd/Roanoke\nnorthward. This is where amounts will push 2-3 inches before\ntapering to showers later in the afternoon. Farther southwest,\ntotal amounts will range from 0.50 to 1.50, with the least \nrainfall occurring west of I-77. \n\nFlood Watch which was issued yesterday has been trimmed to\nreflect just those areas that have the potential to exceed 2\ninches of rain. Even for the flood watch area, any flooding \nwill be of the nuisance variety where leaves have clogged \ndrains and roadside ditches, forcing water onto the road, or \nflooding of areas where drains are backed up. Stream levels were\nat all time lows prior to this rain event, so actual creek and \nriver flooding should be minor. That said, there should be some \nsudden rises on area creeks if basin averages begin to exceed 2 \ninches. For the most part, this will be a beneficial much needed\nsoaking rain. \n\nAs for the wind. That is a different story and will be the\nbiggest hazard in play, especially this morning. Wind gusts of\n35 to 50 mph have been observed, and there is potential for\nupwards of 60 mph gusts along the VA Blue Ridge northeast of the\nRoanoke Valley and into the Highlands of both VA and WV. AEP is\nshowing upwards of 6000 customers without power within the\nservice area this morning and this number may go up as trees and\ntree limbs continue to fall. A significant low level jet (80 kts\nat 850 MB) has developed on the north side of this low pressure\nsystem and this feature will persist until the low moves\noverhead this afternoon. Once the low moves into VA, then the \nwinds should begin to subside with speeds coming down during the\nafternoon. That said, they will redevelop in the wake of the \nstorm system tonight, with a blustery day on tap Friday.\n\nAs for temperatures today...the rain and cloud cover will keep\nthe readings anchored for most of the day favoring readings in\nthe 40s areawide this morning, then maybe recover into the lower\nto mid 50s for the piedmont this afternoon. In general, it will\nbe a raw Thanksgiving morning with the wind driven rain. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Blustery and cold Friday with mountain flurries and snow\nshowers.\n\nAs the low continues to deepen and move north into New England\non Friday, cold air advection will bring temperatures down. Lows\ntonight will be in the 30s and dipping into the 20s for the \nhigher elevations. Highs Friday will range from the 30s \nmountains to 40s piedmont. These colder temperatures will \ncombine with lingering moisture for flurries and snow showers \nacross the western slopes of the Appalachians with potential for\nlight snowfall accumulations for western Greenbrier County, WV,\nand from Burks Garden and Mount Rogers VA southward into the \nNorth Carolina High Country. \n\nCalmer weather returns for the weekend with temperatures \ntrending warmer for Saturday and Sunday. \n\nA cold front is forecast to cross the area late Sunday with an\nopportunity for rain showers. It appears the front will be\nmoisture starved, so rain amounts should be light.",
